{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes replacing Boolean attack with bounded integer weights and a (label, weight) lattice, but this directly violates the problem requirement to PRESERVE the existing semantics. The skeptical fixed-point semantics is defined over three-valued labels (accepted, refuted, suspended); introducing weighted labels and a threshold τ changes the operator's domain and the meaning of 'accepted', so it no longer computes the same grounded extension. The proposal strengthens by REPLACING the semantics rather than EXTENDING it, failing the standard.","counterexample":["Consider an AF with two arguments A and B where A attacks B and B attacks A. Under the standard skeptical fixed point, both are suspended. Under SRC_001's weighted semantics, assign weights so that A's attack on B has weight 2 and B's attack on A has weight 1, with τ=2. Then A is accepted (its defense weight 2 ≥ τ) and B is refuted, changing the grounded extension. This demonstrates the proposal does not preserve the existing semantics."],"target":"10d18e6652e2cc898265363af979941f9a08d15f3c48e6b767a83bdc505adf2b"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"86232ebf5626efc5d29fb5630b9284af34a93dae63929c770bc111d0c4e4e61a"}]}